# General Rules

## Communication
- Be concise and direct. No padding, no summaries of what you just did.
- Challenge requests when better approaches exist — especially statistical correctness. If a check will produce false positives at scale (e.g. raw Z-score on heavy-tailed data), say so and propose the right method.
- If unsure, ask the user rather than guessing.
- User is your helper — ask them to check the UI or console/backend logs when needed.

## Code quality
- No workarounds or fallbacks. Everything through one preferred path. Same for algorithms — one canonical implementation per method, never two.
- Avoid duplicate code — create common helper methods. Every detector returns the same `DetectorResult` shape; every adapter implements the same `WarehouseAdapter` protocol.
- Split source files >1,000 lines.
- No features, refactors, or abstractions beyond what the task requires. Three similar lines beats a premature abstraction.
- No error handling, fallbacks, or validation for scenarios that can't happen. Trust internal code and framework guarantees. Only validate at system boundaries (user input, external APIs).

## Comments
- Default to no comments. Only add one when the WHY is non-obvious: a hidden constraint, a subtle invariant, a workaround for a specific bug, surprising behaviour.
- For statistical methods, link the canonical paper/reference at the top of the file in a single line — that's the spec, the code is the implementation.
- Never write multi-paragraph docstrings or multi-line comment blocks.
- Don't reference the current task, fix, or callers ("used by X", "added for Y flow") — those belong in the PR description and rot.

## After changes
- Offer to run unit tests, git commit, and push — but never commit without explicit permission.
- Don't create dedicated documentation files for each fix/feature. Only create architecture docs for substantial issues, after checking with the user. Algorithm docs are the exception — every detector gets a one-paragraph entry in `docs/algorithms/`.
- After writing any frontend code, run `pnpm build` and fix all errors and warnings before reporting done.
